    q: 'In 2018, Soroush Vosoughi, Deb Roy and Sinan Aral published a study in the journal Science that examined how true and false stories spread on Twitter. ----. One possible explanation, they proposed, is that false stories tend to be more novel and surprising, which makes people more eager to pass them on. If so, the problem may lie less in the technology itself than in what people find exciting.',
